WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.899
C provides multiple integer types, and
each type has a width, signedness, and range.

2
00:00:04.979 --> 00:00:07.581
A value that fits in one type may not fit
in another.

3
00:00:07.661 --> 00:00:11.936
An expression that behaves as intended on
one platform may deserve another review

4
00:00:12.016 --> 00:00:15.982
when compiled under a different data model
or passed through a different API

5
00:00:16.062 --> 00:00:16.619
boundary.

6
00:00:16.699 --> 00:00:21.098
Signed and unsigned types follow different
rules and have different failure modes.

7
00:00:21.178 --> 00:00:25.206
Signed types can represent negative and
positive values within their range.

8
00:00:25.286 --> 00:00:29.685
Unsigned types represent nonnegative
values and have modulo arithmetic behavior.

9
00:00:29.765 --> 00:00:31.871
Neither choice is automatically safer.

10
00:00:31.951 --> 00:00:35.917
The right choice depends on what the value
means and which operations will be

11
00:00:35.997 --> 00:00:36.616
performed.

12
00:00:36.696 --> 00:00:39.856
Fixed-width types, such as explicitly
sized integer types,

13
00:00:39.936 --> 00:00:41.857
can make width assumptions clearer.

14
00:00:41.937 --> 00:00:44.725
They are useful when parsing a wire field,
file field,

15
00:00:44.805 --> 00:00:47.159
or hardware register that has a defined
size.

16
00:00:47.239 --> 00:00:50.833
But a fixed-width type does not remove the
need for range validation.

17
00:00:50.913 --> 00:00:54.878
The program still needs to decide which
values are allowed and whether later

18
00:00:54.958 --> 00:00:56.383
arithmetic stays in range.

19
00:00:56.463 --> 00:01:00.057
The type size_t is commonly used for
object sizes and memory lengths.

20
00:01:00.137 --> 00:01:04.102
It is the right vocabulary for many
allocation and buffer-size interfaces,

21
00:01:04.182 --> 00:01:05.359
but it is still finite.

22
00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:09.405
Code that multiplies an element count by
an element size, adds a terminator,

23
00:01:09.485 --> 00:01:13.946
or combines a header with data length
still needs checked arithmetic before trusting

24
00:01:14.026 --> 00:01:14.645
the result.

25
00:01:14.725 --> 00:01:18.876
Platform data models can affect the width
of int, long, pointer-related types,

26
00:01:18.956 --> 00:01:20.258
and size-related types.

27
00:01:20.338 --> 00:01:22.816
Code that assumes long is always wider
than int,

28
00:01:22.896 --> 00:01:25.994
or that an intermediate expression is
always large enough,

29
00:01:26.074 --> 00:01:27.994
may be less portable than it appears.

30
00:01:28.074 --> 00:01:32.288
Defensive code checks the range required
by the operation rather than trusting a

31
00:01:32.368 --> 00:01:34.288
vague sense that a type is big enough.

32
00:01:34.368 --> 00:01:36.847
Constants, parsed values, external
metadata,

33
00:01:36.927 --> 00:01:40.830
and API parameters should be reviewed for
intended type and allowed range.

34
00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:42.707
A literal constant can have a type.

35
00:01:42.787 --> 00:01:47.186
A parsed number can exceed the business
range even if the parsing function succeeds.

36
00:01:47.266 --> 00:01:51.355
An API may accept one numeric type while
the caller stores the value in another.

37
00:01:51.435 --> 00:01:55.338
Integer safety starts by naming what the
value represents and what range is

38
00:01:55.418 --> 00:01:56.100
acceptable.
